<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anthony the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 251–356</span> · Also known as Saint Anthony the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anthony the Great was an Egyptian Christian who gave away his property and withdrew into the desert as a hermit in the late third century. Disciples gathered around him, and he is regarded as a founder of Christian monasticism. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-antony">Life of Antony</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanasius-of-alexandria">Athanasius of Alexandria</a> made him the model of the ascetic hermit throughout the Christian world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anthony-the-great.jpg" alt="Anthony the Great" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Damaskinos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Anthony_(Damaskinos).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
